
# AquaTrace — Decision Log

## Project purpose
AquaTrace is an academic prototype designed to help a field team track possible water losses and follow each case until the result is verified.

## Primary problem
Detecting a possible loss is not enough. The team needs an owner, a next action, a status, and a follow-up date for every alert.

## Primary user
The pilot coordinator responsible for monitoring cases across 10 households.

## Pilot scope
- Track three simulated water-loss cases.
- Assign a responsible person.
- Record the next action and follow-up date.
- Update case status.
- Distinguish pending, in-progress, verified, and reopened cases.
- Measure verified resolutions instead of counting alerts alone.

## Technology decisions
- HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for the prototype.
- GitHub Pages for public demonstration.
- Simulated data only; no real household information.
- No external API keys or backend required for this demonstration.

## IoT and machine learning
The current interface presents simulated water signals. These signals are not real sensor readings, and the current version does not contain a trained machine-learning model. Any future anomaly-detection demonstration must be clearly labeled as simulated and evaluated before operational use.

## Risks and safeguards
- An alert without a responsible person may never lead to action.
- A detected issue must not be counted as resolved until the outcome is verified.
- Reopened cases and repeat visits must remain visible.
- If repair capacity is unavailable, the system must document that limitation.
- People must not be excluded because they lack a smartphone or respond late.
- Identifiable household information must not be sold.
- Water savings must not be claimed without verification.

## Success definition
The prototype succeeds when a coordinator can select a case, record its status, responsible person, next action, and follow-up date, and see the updated case in the dashboard.

## Next steps
1. Publish the prototype with GitHub Pages.
2. Test the workflow as a pilot coordinator.
3. Identify one usability issue and fix it.
4. Prepare the demo and reflect on limitations.
